    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (80.24) leading the charge as our most potent option. While he faces a formidable arm in Luis Castillo, the combination of his leadoff spot, favorable park factors, and a massive dynamic platoon advantage elevates his floor significantly. Given our organizational priority on stolen bases, **Pete Crow-Armstrong** remains an essential start despite a minor drag from Castillo\u2019s efficiency in holding runners. Supporting him at the top of the order are **Trea Turner** (73.19) and **Kyle Schwarber** (72.52), both of whom benefit from a high-upside matchup against Janson Junk. The proximity of Junk\u2019s xERA (4.51) to our hitters' base projections creates a premium environment for both, particularly **Trea Turner**, who gains a critical +12% bump from the matchup's favorable SP skill metrics.\n\nThe most difficult decision on today\u2019s card was excluding **Chase Meidroth** (48.63) from the starting nine. While he flirted with the marginal threshold, the optimizer ultimately pivoted to **Hector Rodriguez** (49.93) and the stability of our primary infielders. **Chase Meidroth** faced a daunting task against Shota Imanaga, and while his platoon splits provided a slight lift, the negative adjustment for Imanaga\u2019s SP skill suppressed his ceiling just enough to keep him on the bench. Elsewhere, **Caleb Durbin** (70.40) stands out as an elite \"bang for your buck\" play; he capitalizes on a soft matchup against Mitch Bratt, with his score bolstered significantly by the strongest SP skill advantage on the roster (+19%).\n\nWe must remain vigilant regarding the lineup statuses for **Dillon Dingler**, **Christian Encarnacion-Strand**, **Colt Keith**, **Pedro Ramirez**, **Junior Caminero**, **Lars Nootbaar**, **Hector Rodriguez**, **Kyle Schwarber**, and **Carson Benge**. Because these projections are tethered to assumed lineup spots, any late-breaking news\u2014such as **Dillon Dingler** or **Junior Caminero** sliding down the order\u2014would necessitate a re-evaluation of their daily efficiency scores. **Christian Encarnacion-Strand** (55.47) and **Carson Benge** (55.18) sit right at the edge of our marginal category; if weather or late lineup shifts materialize, these two are the first candidates for defensive substitutions to protect our game caps.",
